WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.409
An agent's model samples token ids.

00:00:02.409 --> 00:00:04.636
The harness around it keeps only text.

00:00:04.636 --> 00:00:06.864
So what does the trainer get back?

00:00:09.415 --> 00:00:10.500
Hi, I'm Rasmus!

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.670
Coding agents talk in chat messages.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.840
Reinforcement learning trains on token ids.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.733
Here is how tokin keeps them in step.

00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:20.315
Chapter one.

00:00:20.315 --> 00:00:22.011
Two programs, two languages.

00:00:26.342 --> 00:00:28.324
A coding agent is two programs.

00:00:28.324 --> 00:00:31.958
The model samples ids, and the server decodes them to text.

00:00:32.378 --> 00:00:36.362
The harness, the loop that runs tools and asks again, keeps only that text.

00:00:36.782 --> 00:00:40.301
The next request is text, and the server encodes it again.

00:00:40.301 --> 00:00:42.540
Nothing promises the same ids come back.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.181
But reinforcement learning needs the exact ids the policy sampled, because that

00:00:47.181 --> 00:00:49.291
is what the gradient goes through.

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:51.438
Chapter two.

00:00:51.438 --> 00:00:52.880
Same text, other ids.

00:00:57.342 --> 00:01:01.853
Qwen three point five can spell the word rollouts a hundred and eighty-nine ways.

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:04.109
The model may sample any of them.

00:01:04.109 --> 00:01:05.076
Re-encoding picks one.

00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:10.782
Here is one assistant turn as the model sampled it: a short

00:01:10.782 --> 00:01:12.309
thought, then a tool call.

00:01:12.309 --> 00:01:15.363
Each id carries the log probability it was sampled at.

00:01:15.783 --> 00:01:18.269
Render the next request and encode it again.

00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:21.999
When the model sampled the canonical spelling, the same ids come back.

00:01:22.419 --> 00:01:26.309
But say the model sampled rollout, then s, and a blank line.

00:01:26.309 --> 00:01:30.199
Re-encoding gives roll, then outs, and the template trims the blank line.

00:01:30.199 --> 00:01:33.441
Three ids differ, at the same length, and nothing crashes.

00:01:33.861 --> 00:01:37.743
Add a new user message, and the template drops the old thinking.

00:01:37.743 --> 00:01:42.272
Every later id shifts left, while its recorded log probability stays where it was.

00:01:43.692 --> 00:01:45.643
The chat template rewrites things too.

00:01:45.643 --> 00:01:48.570
The article ran Qwen three point five's own template.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:49.588
It trims.

00:01:49.588 --> 00:01:53.173
A blank line the model sampled before the end of its thinking

00:01:53.173 --> 00:01:54.668
becomes a single line break.

00:01:55.088 --> 00:01:57.798
It rebuilds tool calls in its own canonical form.

00:01:58.218 --> 00:02:02.949
And when a new user message arrives, it deletes earlier thinking from the prompt.

00:02:02.949 --> 00:02:05.315
In the article's example, eighteen ids vanish.

00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:07.941
Here is the formula.

00:02:08.817 --> 00:02:11.259
Policy gradients weight each token by a ratio.

00:02:11.259 --> 00:02:14.923
The bottom was recorded by the sampler, for the id it chose.

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:18.964
The top is recomputed by the trainer, on whatever ids it is handed.

00:02:19.384 --> 00:02:23.075
Hand it re-encoded ids and the ratio compares different tokens.

00:02:23.075 --> 00:02:24.920
Clipping only bounds the error.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.242
And it reinforces a spelling the model never produced.

00:02:30.092 --> 00:02:30.773
Chapter three.

00:02:30.773 --> 00:02:32.136
Token in, token out.

00:02:36.142 --> 00:02:37.742
Here's the idea behind tokin.

00:02:37.742 --> 00:02:39.982
Sit between the harness and the engine.

00:02:39.982 --> 00:02:44.462
Keep every id the model sampled, and encode only the messages that are new.

00:02:46.192 --> 00:02:48.900
So tokin keeps one rule along every trajectory.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.853
The next prompt starts with the last prompt's stored ids.

00:02:52.273 --> 00:02:54.548
Then exactly the ids the model sampled.

00:02:55.125 --> 00:02:56.723
Then delta, the new message.

00:02:56.723 --> 00:02:58.641
That is all tokin ever encodes.

00:03:00.717 --> 00:03:02.809
The launch animation shows one request.

00:03:02.809 --> 00:03:05.249
Attach finds its place in the session.

00:03:05.249 --> 00:03:06.992
Render locks the sampled ids.

00:03:07.412 --> 00:03:09.860
Generate sends ids to the engine and back.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.001
Parse returns a message to the harness.

00:03:13.842 --> 00:03:18.098
The harness sends its conversation as messages to a session address on tokin.

00:03:18.518 --> 00:03:22.732
tokin finds the turn this request continues, and sends the engine its

00:03:22.732 --> 00:03:25.191
stored ids plus the new messages, encoded.

00:03:25.611 --> 00:03:27.895
Sampled ids come back with log probabilities.

00:03:27.895 --> 00:03:32.463
tokin files them in the session's tree and parses a message for the harness.

00:03:32.883 --> 00:03:36.691
Each leaf is a rollout: ids, a loss mask and log

00:03:36.691 --> 00:03:38.076
probabilities, exactly as sampled.

00:03:40.092 --> 00:03:41.954
Harnesses retry, edit and compact.

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:46.421
Here request D rewrote an observation, so it branches at A and

00:03:46.421 --> 00:03:47.910
keeps A's sampled ids.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.103
Every root-to-leaf path is one rollout.

00:03:50.103 --> 00:03:52.171
C and D are this session's two.

00:03:53.842 --> 00:03:54.435
Chapter four.

00:03:54.435 --> 00:03:55.622
Does it help training?

00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:04.117
Trained this way, with slime, the dense model went from one half

00:04:04.117 --> 00:04:06.247
to four fifths on held-out coding tasks.

00:04:07.442 --> 00:04:10.200
The article read the numbers at their sample sizes.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.492
There is no run of the same recipe through a re-rendering gateway,

00:04:14.492 --> 00:04:17.073
so nothing measures what token fidelity is worth.

00:04:17.493 --> 00:04:20.504
The Terminal-Bench gains sit at the edge of their own noise.

00:04:20.924 --> 00:04:25.932
And version zero point one is text only, on SGLang, for four template families.

00:04:27.392 --> 00:04:29.225
Never encode what the model sampled.

00:04:29.225 --> 00:04:31.974
Keep the ids, and encode only what is new.

00:04:34.942 --> 00:04:36.036
Re-encoding changes ids.

00:04:36.036 --> 00:04:38.223
tokin encodes only what is new.

00:04:38.223 --> 00:04:40.046
Every leaf is a rollout.

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:41.140
Thanks for watching!

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.739
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:43.739 --> 00:04:44.362
I'm Rasmus.

00:04:44.362 --> 00:04:44.673
Bye!
